 going to end up and so  what I came away learning is that we're  all weighed down by a kind of anxiety  that's generated in uncertain times and  so this broader societal and security  that we're all feeling plays out in  really important ways in the day to day  lives that families  takes a big psychological toll this is  really interesting to me because to be  clear to the audience this is not a old  look at all the poor rich people who are  also worried about not being rich  anymore you're actually talking about a  concept from behavioral economics or at  least I interpreted it this way you tell  me if I if this is correct of hedonic  adaptation which is that once you sort  of establish a new baseline for a  standard of living or level of income or  wealth or whatever the case may be the  initial sort of satisfaction benefits  that might have come come with it  effectively go away and the new standard  of living becomes a baseline and the  potential loss of that standard of  living is just as anxiety causing as  what you might have experienced at a  lower level of income those are not  that's not the language you apply to it  but I saw that in the way that you  described it is there something to that  it builds on that it's related to it but  it's a little bit distinct so one thing  we should talk about is the shift in  risk that's occurred over the last few  decades so um it used to be that we  shared risk with employers in other  words they would provide benefits and we  would work really hard and use our  talents to help build up a company that  kind of social contract started to  change and over time government and  employers we're really shifting risk  onto individuals and their families you  can think of a quintessential example as  the shift from pensions to 401ks used to  receive pensions now everyone's on the  hook to save whatever money they can and  also bear out the risks of any you know  investments that go wrong so there's  actually been a shift in responsibility  for creating security as well as costs  have gone up exponentially and we don't  have as many middle-class jobs anymore  so when affluent families who are you  know reading the paper and listening to  the news see all of this and they see  how difficult it is out there just to  have a normal middle-class life they get  really concerned about what this means  for their kids and as we know the  economic returns financial returns for  going to college and even graduate  school are so important and so what's  really amping up they're worried is a  sense that you know it's no longer sort  of a done  deal anymore that my kid just goes to  college and then will get a really good  job and they'll be okay that's not  that's not the situation anymore and so  that they're reading those kinds of tea  leaves and saying if I want my kid to  end up where I am something else has to  happen and so in response to all the  insecurities they want to create like an  economic firewall between their family  and this insecurity and that requires a  lot of money and a lot of positioning of  children and I write a lot in the book  about one particular family so much is  focused on making sure children can get  into really top universities because  that is seen as sort of the golden  ticket to protecting them giving them as  much you know educational credentials  and skill sets as possible and so the  anxiety in that project is immense so  I'm certainly not saying oh the poor  rich but it's it's interesting and says  something that even the most advantaged  people and an increasingly unequal  system are really really worried you  mentioned college in education there's a  few different areas that I want to go  into more detail with but since you  mentioned College let's talk about that  a little bit you interviewed more than  50 families for the book there there's a  significant awareness in society that if  college costs sort of continue  increasing at the rate that they've been  increasing and the wage reward for those  degrees does not increase in proportion  to that which it's not in other words  the degree is relatively more expensive  compared to what you would expect to  earn at a certain point some people say  there's going to have to be some kind of  breaking point and it's going to have to  require some kind of major restructuring  or reckoning or simply the the you may  see a move away from even going to  college and a significant decrease in  college enrollment or some kind of major  change how much did that dynamic factor  into the families you talk to conception  of college education and bigger picture  how are you thinking about what what  those numbers sort of point to so among  affluent families college was seen as  the equivalent of high school it was not  seen as something that guaranteed  anything and graduate school was  really really important in fact I  interviewed a young teenage girl who  said to me that her family had so often  talked about college and graduate school  that she didn't realize they were two  different things until right so that's  just sort of a clue and a very revealing  one about the educational standards in  that in that kind of environment among  middle class families there still was  more sense that a college degree is  required they they tended to think it  would lead to a bit more security even  though many of them had college degrees  and we're dealing with some kind of  different economic pressures from the  layoffs to to cuts and pay and things  like that but there wasn't so much  concern about making sure my child gets  a graduate degree what they were really  dealing with was how are we gonna pay  for this what are we gonna do and that  kind of worry on a family kind of  ripples through and you know then  there's the financial aid and figuring  out how to do it and strategizing and  then really also very sad sad stories of  children getting into schools their  dream schools and not being able to go  because there's no way to pay for it  and running the numbers and realizing  you know it's much better if you go to a  local Community College for a bank  account than going to this top school so  what scholars often talk about is the  diverging destinies as as social classes  as we have more inequality and people  move further and further apart from each  other children start to develop  different paths and different outcomes  and so what we're likely to see and what  we're beginning to see in some recent  analysis is that the children in the  kind of top highest earning families  have graduation rates that are much  higher than everybody else I think it's  like the top ten percent of households  their children are graduating over half  of them are graduating finishing college  we're in the bottom 10% it's only 9  percent of those kids so this is really  alarming from a social mobility  perspective and the American dream  whether it's with education or any of  the other areas that you would touch on  with the more than 50 families that you  talk to  did you notice either explicitly or  implicitly I'm kind of worried this the  right way a difference in what they  blamed for the status quo  based on their political leanings in  other words were you able to determine  that those who maybe are more  conservative tended less to blame  government because they expect less from  government like as an example or you  know kind of fill in the blanks in  whatever way you might have observed  that well you know my study was done in  Silicon Valley which is a more  left-leaning place interestingly  politics didn't come to the surface very  much and I think what's happening is I  took a step back is when people  experience insecurity they experience it  as a personal failing they don't they're  not immediately thinking about the  policy failures that led to the outcome  so for example I I got to know a woman  in Laura Delgado who was going through a  divorce and downward mobility when I met  her her life was really falling apart in  a lot of ways and fears before I met her  she had had a seizure and I'm while  driving got in a car accident had you  know thousands of tens of thousands of  dollars in medical bills and when she  talked about that that you know putting  her family in debt she talked about how  we should have had health insurance  well they had forgotten health insurance  because her husband had recently gotten  a job that didn't provide health  insurance so that's a moment where you  can imagine many different responses to  that happening to someone someone's been  saying it's not fair that I don't have  health insurance it's not okay that I  get in an accident that's beyond my  control and not only is it seeking me  but it's gonna think my kids and their  futures interestingly people really  thought about what they could have done  how they could have acted differently  when oftentimes there really wasn't a  different way to act and that really  prevented a political understanding and  political sort of demands on changing  the system it's hard to understand how  we have such high levels of inequality  in this country without people  protesting more or even rioting in the  streets when you actually look at the  numbers and we have some unprecedented  levels going back to you know the Gilded  Age and so people are talking themselves  through this experience they're working  on their emotions they're they're sort  of downplaying their anxiety  middle-class working-class families are  because they really feel like these  problems are so much so big so beyond  them that  to focus on what they individually can  do but by doing that it kind of eclipses  the larger the rod larger dynamics of  policy failures that have created the  problems that they're enduring did you  get a sense of why we're not seeing more  civil disobedience strategic defaults I  mean we've talked a lot on my program  about there is more one can do than just  writing to your elected officials and  signing petitions online that being said  many times people don't feel that it's  bad enough yet to go to that sort of  next level did you get any insight into  what it would take to get to that kind  of next level of protest and civil  disobedience over the over the  structural problems that we're seeing  that's a great question I mean so what I  you know where's the affluent families  my study we're striving for more and  more the working class the middle-class  families are really trying to get used  to less like that's how they were coping  with this is really scaling back their  definitions of security Laura Delgado  said to me you know having nothing isn't  always a bad thing she tried to make  this really difficult situation more  tolerable though their electricity got  turned off because they couldn't pay the  bill and she described the the night  that it happened to me and she said but  it's okay it was like camping because me  and the kids are in the living room you  know huddled under our sleeping bags the  intensity of the economic difficulty is  so hard that people cope in whatever way  they can and I think it become can  become extremely difficult to kind of  link up your individual situation with  these policy failures on a higher order  on so many jobs not having health  insurance on so many jobs not paying  good wages so the moment in which  someone can take their individual  insecurity and link it up with something  in a way that galvanizes them is really  important to understand part of it is  the stories around them and the  frameworks around them that they can use  to make sense of the world and  unfortunately this anti-government  big-government narrative is also  available and sometimes that can leave  people to turn the blame back on  themselves yeah well I mean I'm thinking  as you say that it seems as though there  are there are individuals and  organizations that benefit from people  like the ones you talk to  not making that connection I mean a  great example right now is the idea of  the undocumented immigrant lowering  wages for Americans and the focus on the  immigrant looking to work rather than  the corporation that's so willing to  hire them and it seems that if more  people made the connection maybe that  would catalyze more activism yeah I mean  really understanding how social change  happens how people get highly  politicized is such an important  question to ask when you think about it  it's partly we just need to go back to  history and understand you after the the  Great Depression how did people come to  think that government had an obligation  to give them security provide them with  security really it took you know an  unemployment rate of you know almost a  quarter or whatever it was at the height  of it that Soma it was so much bigger  than any individual person you could no  longer blame yourself and so it's  talking about how of course we can all  make better decisions but it's there  growing in income and wealth inequality  in our country is not because  individuals aren't making the right  decisions it's not because people aren't  working hard it's because the system is  unfair and people can no longer  translate working hard into moving up  and so I think it's providing  alternative frameworks and different  ways of understanding the things that  happen to people but America is a  country deeply rooted in individualism  which has a lot of benefits but it also  can serve just kind of obscure some of  the larger dynamics and social forces  that come down and you know their their  pressures on families you know when a  husband and wife that are having a fight  about you know not enough money they're  not thinking about you know policy trade  policy and how that actually is the the  lard they're wrapping around their fight  absolutely the book is called cut adrift  families and insecure times we've only  touched on some of the topics that Mary  Ann Cooper discusses in the book thanks  so much for talking to me about this  today thanks it's good to be here

